Split out of karr #150, which closed the exposure with a guard on the write path (docs/adr/0046). This is the structural half and it is the wire lane: it moves what the MAC covers.

sops decrypts RULE-first. A leaf the encryption rule says is not encrypted is taken as a LITERAL value and hashed as one, so an ENC[...] string sitting under a rule that excludes it goes into the digest verbatim and the MAC stops the document. Measured on sops 3.13.3: exit 51, MAC mismatch, for every rule shape that excludes an encrypted leaf.

This distribution decrypts ENC-driven: _decrypt_tree does not consult should_encrypt_path at all, so it decrypts every leaf that LOOKS encrypted, whatever the rule says. Two consequences:

  1. We READ, at exit 0, a document sops refuses at exit 51. karr #150 measured the write side of that (rotate wrote the plaintext back out) and now refuses it, in that one direction only.
  2. The other direction is still divergent and is not a leak: a BARE leaf the rule SELECTS. sops refuses it at exit 25 ("Input string <value> does not match sops data format"); here it is read as a literal and, on a re-encryption, silently ENCRYPTED. Measured. Note the legitimate case that any such check has to exclude: sops leaves an empty string and a null bare whatever the rule says, and reads them back at exit 0.

The change: make _decrypt_tree consult should_encrypt_path, take an excluded leaf literally, and let _mac_digest see the ENC[...] text. That is what makes both directions fall out of the MAC rather than out of a guard.

When it lands, _assert_rule_covers_encrypted_leaves and _encrypted_leaves_the_rule_excludes in lib/File/SOPS.pm come out with it, and the pins in t/60 move to the MAC failure. The guard refuses a strict subset of what the MAC would refuse, with one deliberate exception: under ignore_mac => 1 the guard still refuses where the MAC cannot run. Decide there whether ignore_mac should keep refusing.

